---
// src/pages/blog/choosing-bra-manufacturer.astro
import BlogLayout from '../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro';
import Picture from '../../components/Picture.astro';
import BlogSchema from '../../components/BlogSchema.astro';
import { blogSchemaBySlug } from '../../data/blog-schema';

const entry = blogSchemaBySlug['choosing-bra-manufacturer'];
---
<BlogLayout
  title="How to Choose the Right Bra Manufacturer for Your Brand"
  description="A practical guide for DTC brands looking for a reliable bra OEM partner — from MOQ considerations to quality certification requirements."
>
  <article class="container-site py-12 max-w-3xl">
    <nav class="breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span>
      <a href="/blog">Blog</a><span>/</span>
      <span class="text-navy font-medium">Choosing a Bra Manufacturer</span>
    </nav>

    <div class="aspect-[16/10] overflow-hidden rounded-xl bg-cream-dark mb-8">
      <Picture src="blog-choosing-manufacturer.webp" alt="Range of intimate apparel styles a brand sources from an OEM manufacturer" cls="w-full h-full object-cover" priority sizes="(max-width: 768px) calc(100vw - 2rem), 704px" />
    </div>

    <time class="text-sm text-slate">June 1, 2026</time>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-navy mt-2 mb-8">How to Choose the Right Bra Manufacturer for Your Brand</h1>

    <div class="prose prose-stone max-w-none">
      <p class="text-lg text-stone leading-relaxed">
        Finding the right manufacturing partner is one of the most critical decisions for any intimate apparel brand. 
        The wrong choice can lead to quality issues, delayed shipments, and damaged brand reputation. 
        Here's a practical framework for evaluating bra manufacturers.
      </p>

      <h2>0. Before you shortlist anyone, define the product</h2>
      <p>
        Most bad factory relationships start before the first email, because the brand has not
        decided what it is making. A push-up bra describes a category, not a product. Write down
        six things first.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>cup construction - moulded, cut-and-sew or bonded seamless,</li>
        <li>wire or wirefree,</li>
        <li>size range, and whether you need graded cups above DD,</li>
        <li>target landed cost and target retail, which together set your ceiling,</li>
        <li>colourways in the first drop,</li>
        <li>compliance market - EU, US or both, because OEKO-TEX product class and labelling differ.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A factory can quote against that list in an hour. Against a push-up bra they will quote a
        guess, and that guess becomes the anchor for every conversation afterwards.
      </p>

      <h2>1. Start with Certification</h2>
      <p>Any serious bra manufacturer should hold at minimum BSCI (social compliance) and OEKO-TEX Standard 100 (fabric safety) certifications. These aren't just badges — they represent factory conditions, worker welfare, and material safety that directly impact your brand's liability and reputation.</p>
      <p>Ask for the certificate numbers and the expiry dates, not PDFs of logos. Certificates are issued per production site, so a valid BSCI report on a trading company's letterhead tells you nothing about the workshop that will actually run your order.</p>

      <h2>2. Evaluate MOQ Fit</h2>
      <p>Most established bra manufacturers set MOQs between 200-500 pieces per design. If smaller quantities are needed, look for partners with a sample/small batch channel — this allows you to validate quality before scaling.</p>
      <p>The number that matters is not the headline MOQ but the MOQ per colourway. "200 pcs per design" often quietly means 200 per colour, which turns a three-colour launch into a 600-piece commitment before you have sold a single unit.</p>

      <h2>3. Check Category Specialization</h2>
      <p>Not all lingerie factories are equal. Some specialize in wirefree bras using seamless technology, others excel at full-cup underwire constructions. Ensure your factory's core competency matches your product line.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Seamless / wirefree</strong> — bonding machines and circular-knit capability. Ask how many bonding lines they run, because bonded seams cannot be repaired and the equipment is the whole capability.</li>
        <li><strong>Underwire / full cup</strong> — pattern engineering and wire sourcing. Full-cup grading is where most factories quietly fall apart above a DD cup.</li>
        <li><strong>Moulded cup / T-shirt bra</strong> — own mould tooling or outsourced. Outsourced cups mean your lead time is hostage to a third party you never meet.</li>
        <li><strong>Silicone / adhesive</strong> — in-house moulding and adhesive curing, not just assembly of bought-in shells.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>4. Request Samples Before Committing</h2>
      <p>Always request samples before placing bulk orders. Evaluate stitching consistency, fabric hand-feel, hardware quality (adjusters, hooks), and sizing accuracy across the graded range.</p>
      <p>Treat the sample as a manufacturing test, not a look-book. Wash it three times before you judge it — shrinkage, elastic recovery and colour bleed only appear after the first cycle, and those are the three complaints that drive returns.</p>

      <h2>5. Communication &amp; Lead Time</h2>
      <p>The best factory partners respond to technical questions within 24 hours, provide transparent production timelines, and proactively flag potential delays. Typical lead times: 7-10 days for samples, 30-45 days for bulk.</p>
      <p>A useful filter: send one deliberately awkward technical question — say, how they hold a lycra percentage through a three-colour dye lot — and see whether the reply comes from someone who has stood on the production floor.</p>
    </div>

      <h2>6. Price is the last question, not the first</h2>
      <p>
        Comparing quotes before you have compared specifications is the single most common sourcing
        mistake. Two bids that differ by 40% are rarely the same product. Before you look at the
        number, make every supplier quote against the same six-line breakdown.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Fabric and lace</strong>, with the composition and weight stated, not just a name.</li>
        <li><strong>Cup construction</strong>, including whether mould tooling is charged separately and who owns it.</li>
        <li><strong>Trims</strong> - hook-and-eye column count, elastic grade, ring and slider finish.</li>
        <li><strong>Grading</strong>, quoted per size so a wide run is visible rather than hidden in a blended rate.</li>
        <li><strong>Packaging</strong>, because it is the most commonly omitted line and it is often 10-20% of landed cost.</li>
        <li><strong>Testing</strong>, for fibre content, colour fastness and any market-specific requirement.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A quote that cannot be broken down this way is not a quote. It is a number with no
        mechanism behind it, and it will move as soon as the first detail changes.
      </p>

      <h2>7. Ask how they handle a defect</h2>
      <p>
        Every factory ships defects eventually. The variable is whether they tell you. Ask
        directly, in the first call, what their AQL standard is and what happens when a lot fails
        inspection. A supplier who answers with a specific number and a specific remedy - rework,
        credit, or replacement at their cost - has thought about it. A supplier who answers that
        they never have defects has not.
      </p>
      <p>
        Also ask for the last two inspection reports they issued for another client, redacted. The
        way a factory documents its own failures tells you more than any sales deck. For context on
        what those reports should be measuring, see our
        <a href="/blog/oeko-tex-certification">OEKO-TEX guide</a> and
        <a href="/blog/shapewear-manufacturing">shapewear manufacturing breakdown</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What a good partnership looks like after year one</h2>
      <p>
        The relationship that survives is not the one with the lowest first quote. It is the one
        where the factory has your pattern blocks on file, knows which sizes you actually sell,
        flags a fabric discontinuation before it becomes an emergency, and quotes a reorder without
        asking you to resend the tech pack.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the real test of a manufacturing partner: how little work a reorder is. For the pricing
        mechanics behind a first order, see <a href="/blog/bulk-vs-small-batch">bulk versus small
        batch</a>.
      </p>

            <h2>8. What to verify on the factory visit, or on video</h2>
            <p>
              If you cannot visit, ask for a live video walkthrough rather than a photo set. Photos are curated.
              Five things are worth seeing specifically.
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>The cutting room</strong> - is fabric laid and cut by hand or on a machine, and is there a spreader? Hand cutting limits accuracy on lace with a pattern repeat.</li>
              <li><strong>The cup area</strong> - moulding presses present, or boxes of bought-in cups arriving? The second is not disqualifying, but it changes your lead time risk.</li>
              <li><strong>The line</strong> - how many operators, how many operations per garment, and is there a visible in-line check or only an end-of-line one.</li>
              <li><strong>The inspection bench</strong> - a light box, a measurement table and a written AQL sheet on the wall. If the bench does not exist, inspection is happening in the operator's head.</li>
              <li><strong>The sample room</strong> - pattern makers on site. If patterns are outsourced, every fit correction adds a week.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>9. The three questions that reveal the most</h2>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>"Which size do you fit first?"</strong> A factory that answers with your core size understands grading. One that answers with the smallest size does not.</li>
              <li><strong>"Show me a reorder you ran last quarter."</strong> Reorders prove the first order was not the only one that worked.</li>
              <li><strong>"What did you turn down last year, and why?"</strong> A factory with no answer either takes everything or is not being straight with you.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>10. Red flags, in order of how badly they cost you</h2>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>A quote with no breakdown.</strong> You cannot compare it, and it will move once details appear.</li>
              <li><strong>No written AQL standard.</strong> Acceptance becomes whatever the inspector decided that morning.</li>
              <li><strong>MOQ quoted per design but enforced per colour.</strong> Discovered after you have planned a launch around three colourways.</li>
              <li><strong>Pattern making outsourced.</strong> Every fit correction adds a week, and you will do several.</li>
              <li><strong>No current certificate, only a logo on the website.</strong> The most common and the easiest to check.</li>
              <li><strong>Unwillingness to name a reference client.</strong> Not always fatal, but always worth pushing on.</li>
            </ul>

    <div class="mt-12 p-6 bg-cream border-l-4 border-accent">
      <p class="text-sm text-navy font-semibold">Looking for a bra manufacturer?</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-stone mt-1">Charlizi Factory is BSCI & OEKO-TEX certified with MOQ from 200 pcs. <a href="/contact" class="text-accent hover:underline">Send us your inquiry</a> or <a href="https://viomisha.com" target="_blank" class="text-accent hover:underline">try small batch samples on Viomisha</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <BlogSchema slug="choosing-bra-manufacturer" entry={entry} />

  </article>
</BlogLayout>
